// A label is honoured or refused — never ignored.
//
// Named arguments bind against a callee's declared parameter NAMES. A **function value** has none:
// the closure it came from had them, but the `(int, int) -> int` type it flows through carries only
// parameter types, so the call site cannot see them. Unlike a native signature — which opts into
// labels by declaring `ExtFn::param_names` — no registry entry can ever fix this one, which is what
// makes it the durable case to pin here.
//
// It used to be accepted with the label silently discarded, so this call computed `1 - 10 = -9`
// while reading as `10 - 1` — the exact failure named arguments exist to remove.
//
// The check is "a label survived binding": every callee that CAN bind consumes its labels in
// `order_arguments`, so anything still labelled by the time argument checking runs had nothing to
// bind against. Each surviving label is reported on its own, so this call names both.
// expect: error E0061 at 20:8
// expect: error E0061 at 20:14
// expect: exit 1
g = fn(a: int, b: int): int => a - b

echo g(b: 1, a: 10)
